{
  "schema": "rr.site_overlay.v1",
  "snapshot": "april-1343",
  "notes": [
    "Regional site overlay (ADR 0042) for Padise (Padis), the Cistercian house in Harjumaa, April 1343, before the St George's Night attack: the open monastic estate before the quadrangle, its low precinct wall, the grange, the watermill and mill pond on the Kloostri river, fields, woods and the roads east to Harju and Reval, west to Hapsal and south to Pernau.",
    "Coordinates are metres from the origin (the centre of the later monastery ruin, OSM relation 16395411), x east, y south. Every record id is prefixed padise. (ADR 0042 section 5). Points of interest that the Padise monastery controller reads carry the greybox anchor id unchanged in `anchor_id`.",
    "Period rule: the fortified quadrangle, the stone church (consecrated 1448), the gate and gun towers and the moat are later than 1343 and are excluded. Villu Kadakas (AVE 2011, history/AVE2011_Kadakas_Padise.pdf) dates the northern, eastern and southern ranges to about 1350-1400 and places only two masonry buildings before them: the stone house later buried under the western range and the building with arched niches south of it. Everything else here is timber and an uncertainty-aware reconstruction.",
    "OSM supplies the course of the Kloostri river, the river crossing (modern Kloostri sild), the Keila-Haapsalu and Viikingi tee road lines and the ruined manor watermill used as the mill site; they are traced into the overlay records below. Modern manor buildings, the distillery, the park and the gate tower are not used.",
    "The builder has no separate pond record: the mill pond is the widened river reach above the mill (stream widths), a plausible composite of a Cistercian mill dam.",
    "No precinct wall and no paved yard: the pipeline draws free-standing walls as crenellated curtains and a site space as market paving, both of which read as the later fortified, urban monastery. Kadakas describes an open estate in 1343, so the close is open ground; the cart-gate anchor marks where the lane enters it.",
    "Terrain is the EU-DEM trend and the river cut only (terrain.relief false): the shared open-country swells put a 2-3 m hollow under the monastery plateau."
  ],
